WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:06.700
Giselle is back for an Enchanted sequel that's
overflowing with deep-cut Disney easter eggs,

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:08.430
magical references, and in-jokes.

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:13.500
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and I've
been re-watching and pausing Disenchanted

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.020
to uncover all the incredible little details
you might have missed!

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:22.180
Disenchanted begins with a soaring view through
animated Andalasia, where we get a nod to

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:27.160
Sleeping Beauty's royalty, and we get the
first of many Beauty and the Beast references

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:31.429
with a girl reading a book by a fountain surrounded
by a flock of sheep.

00:00:31.429 --> 00:00:37.190
Meanwhile, the firefly that lights up Pip's
home is a nod to firefly Ray who sang "Ma

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:39.480
Belle Evangeline" in The Princess and the
Frog.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:45.430
As soon as Giselle casts the spell that turns
Monroeville into the fairytale land of Monrolasia,

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:48.180
the Disney easter eggs flow thick and fast.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:53.320
So, when Giselle's kitchen cutlery, utensils
and appliances come to life and begin to sing,

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.090
it's a homage to the iconic "Be Our Guest".

00:00:56.090 --> 00:01:00.660
The broom that comes to life with yellow bristle
legs is a tribute to the magical broom Mickey

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.800
brought to life in Fantasia.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.180
And I love the funny little detail of how
the broom is simply sweeping dust under the

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:11.680
rug, rather than cleaning it away, a metaphor
perhaps for how Giselle is trying to sweep

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.330
away her own problems with her magic wishing
wand, rather than dealing with them.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:19.290
When Pip discovers he can talk in the live-action
world...

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:20.290
Wait, how am I talking?

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:24.500
... it's a little joke about the original
Enchanted where he could only squeak in New

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:27.890
York and had to act out what he wanted to
say.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:29.689
And when he exclaims...

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:30.689
Jumpin' jelly sticks!

00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:31.689
We got magicked?

00:01:31.689 --> 00:01:35.840
... that's a reference to Sebastian's surprised
remark in The Little Mermaid when Ariel comes

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:37.430
across a ship.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:39.369
Jumpin' jellyfish!

00:01:39.369 --> 00:01:44.119
Giselle's three gardeners are transformed
into a trio of fairies, as a hat-tip to Aurora's

00:01:44.119 --> 00:01:46.080
three fairy godmothers.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.040
And when wicked stepmother Giselle orders
them to look after her baby Sofia...

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:51.040
You three.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.029
You look like you're good with children.

00:01:53.029 --> 00:01:57.070
My precious darling needs tending to.
...it's a spin on Sleeping Beauty where the

00:01:57.070 --> 00:02:03.030
three fairies raised baby Aurora as they tried
to protect her from Maleficent's curse.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:08.530
And Malvina Monroe's fairytale villain has
strong vibes of the Evil Queen from Snow White,

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:14.099
from her black costume, to her spiky crown,
and of course she even has her own magic mirror

00:02:14.099 --> 00:02:18.519
which is almost a replica of the original
from 1937.

00:02:18.519 --> 00:02:23.290
Morgan's fairytale bedroom is located in the
tower and has boarded-up windows, which is

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:27.790
a reference to the prison-like tower where
Mother Gothel kept Rapunzel hidden away.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:32.360
It also has an extra staircase, similar to
one at Rapunzel's.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:36.930
And there's a spinning wheel with a spindle
like the one that cursed Aurora in Sleeping

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.569
Beauty, and which Rapunzel also had.

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:44.390
Morgan later escapes from the tower using
bedsheets in a riff on how Rapunzel leaves

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:45.390
her tower.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:50.330
The painted walls of Morgan's original room
are also a nod to how Rapunzel painted her

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:51.330
walls.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:56.040
And when Giselle locks Morgan inside her room,
it's a nod to how Lady Tremaine locked Cinderella

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.110
away in the attic.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:03.239
And when Giselle forbids Morgan from going
to the festival because she has too many chores...

00:03:03.239 --> 00:03:04.370
What about the carpets in the hall?

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:05.370
And the windows?

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:06.370
I can't see a thing.

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:07.680
There's the gardening and the sewing and the
mending.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.200
And don't forget about the chimneys.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.200
Filthy business.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:16.121
... the list of things she gives her to do
is almost identical to the one Lady Tremaine

00:03:16.121 --> 00:03:17.540
gives to Cinderella.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:18.950
There's the large carpet.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:19.950
And the windows.

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:21.170
And don't forget the garden.

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:22.170
Clean the chimneys.

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:25.160
And, of course, there's the mending and the
sewing and the laundry.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:30.330
There's another tribute to Cinderella when
Giselle's wish transforms Pip into a cat.

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:32.530
Stepmothers don't have cute chipmunks as friends.

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:33.700
They have--
Cats!

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:35.269
[meows]
And evil ones!

00:03:35.269 --> 00:03:40.580
This is a send-up of Disney's wicked feline
trope and specifically Lucifer, Lady Tremaine's

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:41.580
malevolent pussycat.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:47.690
By the way, I think the animators got Pip's
cat mannerisms down perfectly, from his fascination

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:53.000
with the magic Scroll's tassels, to how he
hilariously tries to squeeze into an impossibly

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.280
small box.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:56.130
You know that's not a litter box.

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:57.510
That's where I live.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:02.010
When Scroll reveals that at midnight Giselle's
spell will become permanent, it's a twist

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:07.060
on the plot of Cinderella where, instead,
the Fairy Godmother's magic disappears on

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:08.610
the stroke of midnight.

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:11.150
It's pretty on the nose, but it's a classic!

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:14.420
The three mean girls who make fun of Morgan
in Monrolasia and also in real-life are a

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:19.300
riff on the three girls who disliked Belle
in both the animated and live-action Beauty

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:20.410
and the Beast movies.

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:26.440
Later on, they also fawn over Tyler, similar
to how the trio were obsessed with Gaston.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:31.531
And as Morgan begins her song "Perfect", the
melody has a real ring to it of "Belle", the

00:04:31.531 --> 00:04:34.280
opening song from Beauty and the Beast.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:38.410
And like Belle, this is also Morgan's big
"I Want" song.

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:43.009
And the splash of water behind Morgan as she
perches on the boxes of fruit is a nod to

00:04:43.009 --> 00:04:48.750
the wave crashing on the rocks behind Ariel
at the end of her "I Want" song "Part of Your

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:49.750
World".

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:54.419
During the villain song "Badder", we get a
peek inside Malvina's cabinet of evil objects

00:04:54.419 --> 00:04:57.130
that she plans to use against Giselle.

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:01.490
Among the villainous paraphernalia is Maleficent's
cursed spinning wheel;

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:06.310
the Enchantress's Enchanted Rose;
the Wicked Queen's poisoned apple;

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:11.050
a "Drink Me" potion bottle like the one that
shrunk Alice in Wonderland;

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:16.169
and this hourglass might be a reference to
the one in which Jafar trapped Jasmine.

00:05:16.169 --> 00:05:21.440
Unlike Giselle or Malvina, you don't need
a magic wand or fairytale fight to get yourself

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:26.400
a fancy title, as thanks to the sponsor of
this video Established Titles, you can become

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.770
your very own Lord or Lady.

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:32.919
Established Titles is based on a historic
Scottish custom where landowners are referred

00:05:32.919 --> 00:05:37.960
to as Lairds, so you can buy a piece of dedicated
land in Scotland which allows you to officially

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:39.930
call yourself a Lord or Lady.

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:44.669
You can use your new title on credit cards,
plane tickets, even dating profiles.

00:05:44.669 --> 00:05:49.030
It's also a fun way to preserve the natural
woodlands of Scotland while helping global

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:54.180
reforestation efforts, as with every order,
Established Titles plants a tree.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:58.919
Title packs give you at least 1 square foot
of dedicated land with a unique plot number

00:05:58.919 --> 00:06:03.990
on a private estate in Eddleston, Scotland
and an official certificate with a crest.

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:08.350
With the holiday season coming up, it's a
great novel present for your family or friends,

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:12.550
and there are even Couple Title packs available
for partners so you can get adjoining plots

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:13.840
of land.

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:18.789
Established Titles is running a massive early
Black Friday Sale right now, plus if you use

00:06:18.789 --> 00:06:22.440
the code FLICKS10 you get an additional 10%
off.

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:27.900
So, go to EstablishedTitles.com/FLICKS10 to
get your gifts now.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:32.610
And there are lots of great Disney easter
eggs among the names of Monrolasia's stores.

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:38.009
You'll be able to spot "Beauty and the Book";
"Painting Painting On The Wall", a magic mirror

00:06:38.009 --> 00:06:40.630
reference;
and a grocers called "A Whole Food World",

00:06:40.630 --> 00:06:44.650
a pun on Jasmine and Aladdin's duet "A Whole
New World".

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:48.470
As Giselle's song continues, we can see "Smee's
Cheese";

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:53.900
and "Le Chapeau Magique" is French for magic
hat, a fun tribute to The Sorcerer's Hat from

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:54.900
Fantasia.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:59.669
Over here is "Fée Marraine", the French name
for the Fairy Godmother in Cinderella;

00:06:59.669 --> 00:07:03.410
and there's another Cinders references with
the "Bibidi Bobidi Butchers";

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:07.130
and there's a Mary Poppins shout-out with
"Mary Popover's Bakery".

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:12.190
And when Giselle goes in search of her missing
wand you can see "Maid Marian's Dairy";

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:14.300
and "Lumiere's Candle Works".

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:19.690
Appropriately enough, during Morgan's "Perfect"
song, you can see a "Practically Perfect Pottery".

00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:24.949
When Morgan activates Giselle's memory tree
in Andalasia, she touches it and as it lights

00:07:24.949 --> 00:07:30.889
up it transports her and Nancy up to Monrolasia,
and the golden lights then fly into Morgan's

00:07:30.889 --> 00:07:35.810
room where they reactivate her own memory
tree, something that Giselle foreshadowed

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:37.310
at the start of the movie.

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:39.720
This isn't anything a little glitter couldn't
fix.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:46.039
The song "Love Power" that Nancy sings has
some really strong Frozen vibes with the swirling

00:07:46.039 --> 00:07:51.270
magical memories and lights reminding me a
lot of Elsa's song "Into The Unknown".

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:56.570
Idina Menzel is, of course, the voice of Elsa
in the Frozen movies, and lines like "Let

00:07:56.570 --> 00:08:01.330
it grow" and "Let it glow" in Nancy's big
power ballad are a winky reference to "Let

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:02.389
It Go".

00:08:02.389 --> 00:08:08.009
During the song, Morgan's torn-up dress transforms
into a beautiful new one, which is Disenchanted's

00:08:08.009 --> 00:08:13.159
take on the Fairy Godmother's transformation
of Cinderella's rags into a ball gown.

00:08:13.159 --> 00:08:17.509
The dresses that Malvina's sidekicks Ruby
and Rosaleen wear for the night of the festival

00:08:17.509 --> 00:08:22.220
are very similar to the ones Cinderella's
nasty stepsisters wore to the ball.

00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:27.220
Director Adam Shankman has said that Malvina's
minions "are sort of an echo of the step-sisters

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:28.660
from Cinderella".

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:32.880
When Giselle and Morgan go dress shopping
for the festival, pause the scene here and

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:37.479
you can spot this film's take on the Enchanted
Rose from Beauty and the Beast together with

00:08:37.479 --> 00:08:41.159
a nod to Belle's iconic yellow ballroom dress
from that movie.

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:47.160
Also, on the right, is a giant red apple,
a reference to the Evil Queen's poisoned apple.

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:50.660
And the costume next to it has shades of Snow
White's outfit.

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:54.600
The colours don't match exactly, but there's
a yellow skirt, the top is a similar style

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:59.470
to the original, and the red shawl feels like
a nod to the inside of Snow White's cape.

00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:04.579
As for Morgan's blue dress, that's a shout-out
to Cinderella's ballroom dress.

00:09:04.579 --> 00:09:09.950
And the way wicked stepmother Giselle cuts
up Morgan's dress so she can't go to the festival

00:09:09.950 --> 00:09:14.570
is a hat-tip to how Cinderella's stepsisters
destroyed the ballroom dress she made for

00:09:14.570 --> 00:09:16.450
herself in the animated movie.

00:09:16.450 --> 00:09:17.710
It's torn to shreds.

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:20.760
Why, it's hardly fit for a maid.

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:25.500
Plus Giselle calling her stepdaughter a maid
is another Cinderella reference.

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:30.440
And when Giselle realises what she's turning
into, she demonstrates a pose for Pip which

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:35.200
I think has a vibe of Disney's OG wicked stepmother,
the Evil Queen.

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:36.200
Oh, actually.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:37.200
Yeah.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:38.200
I can totally see it.

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:43.269
As the movie reaches its climax, vines from
Andalasia begin to take over Monrolasia, covering

00:09:43.269 --> 00:09:48.490
every building in sight, which echoes how
at the end of Sleeping Beauty Maleficent grew

00:09:48.490 --> 00:09:54.020
a forest of thorny vines over the castle to
try and stop Prince Phillip from reaching

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:55.020
Aurora.

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:59.290
And in fact, both Robert and Tyler cut their
way through the vines, similar to how the

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:01.360
Prince does in Sleeping Beauty.

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:07.209
And to match how the vines were taking over
in the grand finale, Giselle's luxurious ballgown

00:10:07.209 --> 00:10:12.000
was designed to give the effect that leaves
and vines were growing out of it so it reflects

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.910
the "final stage of [her] evil metamorphosis".

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:19.680
Although Giselle is broken out of her curse
through the memory tree, Malvina captures

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:25.570
Morgan and gives her a sleeping potion sending
her into a deep slumber like Aurora in Sleeping

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:26.570
Beauty.

00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:29.730
Morgan is freed when Giselle gives up the
magic wand;

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:33.720
however, she proves she's a true daughter
of Giselle and Andalasia when she takes the

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:35.620
broken wand and says...

00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:36.760
I wish I was home...

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:39.660
with my mom.
... which breaks the curse and she then wakes

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:41.390
up at home in her own bed.

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:46.030
All of which is a shout-out to The Wizard
of Oz and how Dorothy wished herself home

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:50.550
at the end of that movie, leaving the fantasy
land of Oz behind and finding herself back

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:53.170
at home in her bed in the real world.

00:10:53.170 --> 00:10:58.339
After the curse is lifted, Giselle's dress
has a clam-shell buckle, a nice call-out to

00:10:58.339 --> 00:11:03.000
The Little Mermaid and Ariel, another Disney
princess who was a big inspiration for Giselle's

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:04.140
character.

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:08.889
An interesting little detail which connects
the magic of Andalasia and Monrolasia throughout

00:11:08.889 --> 00:11:13.820
the movie is the purple and white flower at
the top of the Andalasian wishing wand, known

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:18.320
as the Purple Ballerina, and it appears in
the opening scene where Pip is reading the

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:19.940
story of the movie.

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:24.440
Later, Morgan walks through a clearing in
the Monrolasian woods that is full of them;

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:27.390
and they also hang from the enchanted wishing
well.

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:32.380
The flower foreshadows how Monrolasia will
pull the magic out of Andalasia by the end

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:33.630
of the film.

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:38.600
Although Robert's daughter Morgan was recast
in the sequel, the actress Rachel Covey from

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:43.780
the original movie does have a small but sweet
cameo as a Monrolasian girl who reminds Giselle

00:11:43.780 --> 00:11:45.780
about the festival later that day.

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:46.780
What's tonight?

00:11:46.780 --> 00:11:49.420
[villagers gasp]
The festival, milady.

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:53.490
And there's another cool cameo at the start
of the film in New York City with one of the

00:11:53.490 --> 00:11:54.490
movers.

00:11:54.490 --> 00:11:55.490
All right.

00:11:55.490 --> 00:11:56.590
Next stop:
"Monroeville."

00:11:56.590 --> 00:12:01.459
That's actor Matt Servitto from Enchanted
who played one of the workers in Time Square

00:12:01.459 --> 00:12:06.000
that were repeatedly surprised by visitors
from Andalasia arriving through the manhole.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:11.000
So, what did you think of Disenchanted and
did you notice any other easter eggs?

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:12.680
Let me know in the comments below.

00:12:12.680 --> 00:12:17.870
If you love Disney movies, tap here for another
video just like this, and if you enjoyed this

00:12:17.870 --> 00:12:21.149
video, do leave a thumbs-up, I really appreciate
it.

00:12:21.149 --> 00:12:25.209
Thanks for enjoying this movie with me and
hope you have a marvellous movie-loving week!

00:12:25.209 --> 00:12:26.709
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

